{"kind":"report","text":"Exact-head review of cb1767dddf08b2d518e97ad7333053c2ba05e835, resting on all 70 artifacts, primary packages/client/README.md first. Changes requested.\n\nArchitecture: the fixes for review 80d3710c are correct. Lane/landing revisions prevent value-equality ABA; the act's ownership marker makes its own completed local write safe to retry; credential installations bind file, lease and installation identity. I independently repeated the same-lane lease replacement and X→Y(interrupted)→Z→X recovery cases: the newer credential, mapping and selection survive, without replaying the remote release or acquiring fresh authority. Earlier transport, signing, bearer, attention, validation and recovery fixes remain byte-identical. All 61 unchanged artifacts were checked against revision 4; the nine changes were read in full. All 41 changed lock descriptors exactly match approved lane B f57b6c8, with no removed descriptors.\n\nTwo P2 ownership/durability gaps remain:\nE5.1 [P2] Fence workspace installation itself against an older in-flight response (cli/src/main.ts workspace, around 606-626). The installation ID is generated only AFTER workspaceToken returns; every completion then unconditionally overwrites the shared credential and mapping. A workspace request for lease 1 can receive its valid grant at the server but pause in transit, while release, reclaim of the SAME lane as lease 2, and a newer workspace all finish locally. When the old response resumes, it writes the revoked lease-1 token over the valid lease-2 credential, replaces the lease-2 mapping with lease 1, and reports success; the Room still holds lease 2. Independent HTTP/CLI harness reproduction confirmed all of those outcomes. The same race can put one lane's remote/credential into a repository now selected for another lane. Record local workspace operation/installation ownership before asynchronous work and allow installation only while that ownership and intended lease still match; a superseded completion must keep the newer remote, credential, mapping and selection. Include overlap across same/different lanes, release and failed/partial installations. A final asynchronous read alone does not establish an atomic local ownership boundary.\n\nE5.2 [P2] Version or migrate the persisted local journal/config schema (cli/src/config.ts Store.entry/read; main.ts applyLocal). Journal entries still declare v:1, but select/follow intents changed from expect to rev, release intents changed from credential to installed/lease/revisions, workspace mappings changed from strings to objects, and credential markers changed. Existing entries are merely cast to the new TypeScript types. Independent test loaded the exact revision-4 v:1 answered release intent {kind:'release-lane',lane,credential} with its kept receipt: every retry fails accessing undefined installed.file, retains the unfinished journal and leaves the credential. Old claim intents can instead skip their local step and delete the journal because rev is undefined. Preserve receipt recovery across this persistent-format change: use a distinguishable new schema, explicitly decode supported old entries/config/markers, and apply only ownership that can be proved from their saved evidence. Where old evidence cannot prove a local change is safe, return the retained receipt with a clear conservative/manual-local-step result while preserving newer work; do not infer authority by reissuing the act or silently reinterpret it. Test old prepared/answered entries, old workspace mappings/markers, crash recovery during migration and unknown versions.\n\nSecurity: the original newer-lease deletion is fixed, but E5.1 lets an obsolete completion replace valid local credentials and can misdirect later Git commands. Credential contents remained private and absent from test output; the independent test confirms the installed old token is revoked. E5.2 concerns durable recovery and ownership, not a demand for fresh authority to replay receipts.\n\nSimplification: use one local ownership scheme for BOTH response installation and recovered acts, with an explicit persistent decoder/version. Keep the centralized setters and applyLocal; avoid solving each response order through a special case.\n\nValidation: npm ci installed 352 packages; root strict typecheck passed. Candidate client 86 Node + 2 workerd, CLI 57, MCP 66 Node + 1 workerd passed. Four independent CLI diagnostics passed: two positive regressions and two assertions of the remaining incorrect behavior; the CLI typecheck also passed with the diagnostics. The 942.0 KiB CLI bundle built, bundled help ran, and four package dry-runs verified executable/export presence. No live Room deployment or external mutation was performed. Reproductions are in /tmp/artroom-checker-clients-v5/packages/cli/test/checker-v5-review.test.ts; source work remains untouched.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:70216451610742a21d2034c9733052eb79f610de","binding":"assigned","head":"cb1767dddf08b2d518e97ad7333053c2ba05e835","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7f589a13294813ece8b07f78feed5b766408e889\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3f934a98fa8efcc34c1695fee9b59e58568876e3\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7f589a13294813ece8b07f78feed5b766408e889","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}